  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Given that $x$ and $(x+4)$ are factors of $f(x)=a{x}^{3}+11{x}^{2}-4x+b,$ determine $a+b.$",
  "worked_solution": "According to the factor theorem, if $(x-r)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ then the root $x=r$ is also a root of $f(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering the factors $x$ and $(x+4).$ The roots of these factors can be found by setting the factors equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0 & \\,⇒\\,x=0 \\\\ x+4=0 & \\,⇒\\,x=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the factor theorem, then, since $x$ and $(x+4)$ are factors of $f(x),$ the roots $x=0$ and $x=-4$ are also roots of $f(x).$ This means $f(0)=0$ and $f(-4)=0,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =0 \\\\ a(0)^{3}+11(0)^{2}-4(0)+b & =0 \\\\ b & =0, \\\\  \\\\ f(-4) & =0 \\\\ a(-4)^{3}+11(-4)^{2}-4(-4)+b & =0 \\\\ -64a+176+16+b & =0 \\\\ -64a+192+0 & =0 \\\\ -64a & =-192 \\\\ a & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our solutions are $a=3$ and $b=0.$ Finally then,\n\n$$\na+b=3+0=3.\n$$",
